{"page": 43, "text": "The Shale segment had a gain on commodity derivative instruments - cash settlements of $49 million for the three months ended September 30, 2026 compared to a gain of $21 million for the three months ended September 30, 2025. The notional amounts associated with these financial hedges represented approximately 109.4 Bcf of the Company's produced Shale gas sales volumes for the three months ended September 30, 2026 at an average gain of $0.45 per Mcf hedged. For the three months ended September 30, 2025, these financial hedges represented approximately 115.7 Bcf at an average gain of $0.18 per Mcf hedged.\nThe $0.10 per Mcfe increase in total average Shale sales price was primarily due to a $0.91 per Mcfe increase in the average sales price of NGLs and a $0.22 per Mcf increase in the gain on commodity derivative instruments - cash settlements. These increases were offset, in part, by a $0.22 per Mcf decrease in the average sales price of natural gas.\nTotal operating costs and expenses for the Shale segment were $216 million for the three months ended September 30, 2026 compared to $235 million for the three months ended September 30, 2025. The decrease in total dollars and unit costs for the Shale segment were due to the following items:\n\u2022Shale lease operating expenses were $17 million for the three months ended September 30, 2026 compared to $21 million for the three months ended September 30, 2025. The decrease in total dollars and unit costs was primarily due to lower\n36\nwater disposal costs as more water was reused in well completion activities rather than taken to disposal. The decrease was offset, in part, by lower total sales volumes during the period.\n\u2022Shale production, ad valorem and other fees were $5 million for the three months ended September 30, 2026 compared to $6 million for the three months ended September 30, 2025. The decrease in total dollars and unit costs was primarily due to lower realized natural gas prices. The decrease in unit costs was offset, in part, by lower total sales volumes during the period.\n\u2022Shale transportation, gathering and compression costs were $81 million for both the three months ended September 30, 2026 and 2025. The increase in unit costs was primarily due to lower total sales volumes during the period.\n\u2022Depreciation, depletion and amortization costs attributable to the Shale segment were $113 million for the three months ended September 30, 2026 compared to $127 million for the three months ended September 30, 2025. These amounts included depletion on a units of production basis of $0.66 per Mcfe and $0.74 per Mcfe, respectively. The remaining depreciation, depletion and amortization costs were either recorded on a straight-line basis or related to asset retirement obligations.\n"}
