{"page": 54, "text": "* NGLs and Oil/Condensate are converted to Mcfe at the rate of one barrel equals six Mcf based upon the approximate relative energy content of oil and natural gas, which is not indicative of the relationship of oil, NGLs, condensate, and natural gas prices. The decrease in total Shale sales volumes was primarily due to normal production declines offset, in part, by new wells turned-in-line throughout the last quarter of 2025 and throughout 2026.\nThe Shale segment had natural gas, NGLs and oil/condensate revenue of $1,365 million for the nine months ended September 30, 2026 compared to $1,326 million for the nine months ended September 30, 2025. The $39 million increase was primarily due to a 5.2% increase in the average sales price for natural gas and a 12.2% increase in the average sales price of NGLs. These increases were offset, in part, by a 7.1% increase in the loss on commodity derivative instruments and a 3.7% decrease in total Shale sales volumes.\nThe Shale segment had a loss on commodity derivative instruments - cash settlements of $117 million for the nine months ended September 30, 2026 compared to a loss of $115 million for the nine months ended September 30, 2025. The notional amounts associated with these financial hedges represented approximately 323.7 Bcf of the Company's produced Shale gas sales volumes for the nine months ended September 30, 2026 at an average loss of $0.36 per Mcf hedged. For the nine months ended September 30, 2025, these financial hedges represented approximately 339.0 Bcf at an average loss of $0.34 per Mcf hedged.\nThe $0.19 per Mcfe increase in total average Shale sales price was primarily due to a $0.15 per Mcf increase in average gas sales price and a $0.45 per Mcfe increase in the average NGL sales price. These increases were offset, in part, by a $0.02 per Mcf change in the loss on commodity derivative instruments - cash settlements.\nTotal operating costs and expenses for the Shale segment were $658 million for the nine months ended September 30, 2026 compared to $682 million for the nine months ended September 30, 2025. The decrease in total dollars and increase in unit costs for the Shale segment were due to the following items:\n\u2022Shale lease operating expenses were $47 million for the nine months ended September 30, 2026 compared to $59 million for the nine months ended September 30, 2025. The decrease in total dollars and unit costs was primarily due to lower water disposal costs as more water was reused in well completion activities rather than taken to disposal. The per unit decrease was offset, in part, by the overall decrease in total sales volumes.\n46\n\u2022Shale production, ad valorem and other fees were $17 million for the nine months ended September 30, 2026 compared to $20 million for the nine months ended September 30, 2025. The decrease in total dollars was primarily due to lower Shale gas sales volumes, while unit costs remained flat in the period-to-period comparison.\n"}
