{"page": 56, "text": "The total average CBM sales price increased $0.36 per Mcf due to a $0.33 per Mcf increase in average natural gas sales price and a $0.03 per Mcf decrease in the loss on commodity derivative instruments - cash settlements. The notional amounts associated with these financial hedges represented approximately 21.5 Bcf of the Company's produced CBM sales volumes for the nine months ended September 30, 2026 at an average loss of $0.32 per Mcf hedged. For the nine months ended September 30, 2025, these financial hedges represented approximately 21.8 Bcf at an average loss of $0.36 per Mcf hedged.\nTotal operating costs and expenses for the CBM segment were $112 million for the nine months ended September 30, 2026 compared to $115 million for the nine months ended September 30, 2025. The decrease in total dollars and unit costs for the CBM segment were due to the following items:\n\u2022CBM lease operating expenses were $19 million for the nine months ended September 30, 2026 compared to $18\n47\nmillion for the nine months ended September 30, 2025. The increase in total dollars and unit costs was primarily due to an increase in repairs and maintenance expense.\n\u2022CBM production, ad valorem and other fees were $5 million for both the nine months ended September 30, 2026 and 2025. The increase in unit costs was primarily due to higher realized natural gas prices.\n\u2022CBM transportation, gathering and compression costs were $44 million for the nine months ended September 30, 2026 compared to $49 million for the nine months ended September 30, 2025. The decrease in total dollars and unit costs was due to a decrease in electrical compression rates in the current period.\n\u2022Depreciation, depletion and amortization costs attributable to the CBM segment were $44 million for the nine months ended September 30, 2026 compared to $43 million for the nine months ended September 30, 2025. These amounts included depletion on a unit of production basis of